1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,648
Two economists compute the same statistic on the same data, and get

2
00:00:04,748 --> 00:00:09,467
six point zero. One concludes that the series broke. The other finds

3
00:00:09,567 --> 00:00:13,436
no evidence of a break. Both are right. The data are the

4
00:00:13,536 --> 00:00:16,696
same; what differs is how the date was chosen.

5
00:00:17,366 --> 00:00:19,029
To see why, we go hunting for a

6
00:00:19,129 --> 00:00:21,759
break in sixty-five years of American inflation.

7
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:26,948
Here is US inflation since nineteen sixty, month by month, with a

8
00:00:27,048 --> 00:00:31,227
yearly average in blue. Low in the sixties, high in the seventies,

9
00:00:31,327 --> 00:00:35,507
low again after the early eighties. Did its mean change, and when?

10
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:39,398
Pick a candidate date, T one. Average the months before it

11
00:00:39,498 --> 00:00:43,028
and the months after it. The Wald statistic is the gap

12
00:00:43,128 --> 00:00:47,734
between the two means, squared, measured against its standard error. A

13
00:00:47,834 --> 00:00:51,095
large W says the two halves do not share one mean.

14
00:00:52,166 --> 00:00:55,903
Now let the cursor hunt. At every admissible date we split,

15
00:00:56,003 --> 00:00:59,154
compute W, and draw it below. We do not search the

16
00:00:59,254 --> 00:01:03,121
first and last fifteen percent: there, one mean would rest on

17
00:01:03,221 --> 00:01:07,478
a handful of months, and noise alone would produce huge statistics.

18
00:01:07,566 --> 00:01:11,022
In the mid seventies W almost vanishes: that split

19
00:01:11,122 --> 00:01:14,720
leaves high inflation on both sides. Then it climbs,

20
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:18,205
and peaks in July nineteen eighty-two, at the end

21
00:01:18,305 --> 00:01:22,330
of the Volcker disinflation, with W equal to eighty-eight.

22
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:27,404
Why does the hunt need a higher bar? Take series with no break

23
00:01:27,504 --> 00:01:31,707
at all, and hunt anyway. Each wandering line is W along one noise

24
00:01:31,807 --> 00:01:36,076
series. At any one date it crosses three point eight four only one

25
00:01:36,176 --> 00:01:40,445
time in twenty. But we keep the highest point of the whole search,

26
00:01:40,545 --> 00:01:43,623
and that lands above it about four times in ten.

27
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:47,649
Repeat the hunt a thousand times and collect the highest points.

28
00:01:47,749 --> 00:01:51,192
Their histogram sits far to the right of the chi squared

29
00:01:51,292 --> 00:01:55,304
curve that serves a single date. Its ninety-five percent point is

30
00:01:55,404 --> 00:01:58,594
about eight point seven, not three point eight four.

31
00:01:59,666 --> 00:02:03,351
Our real peak, eighty-eight, clears even the higher bar by

32
00:02:03,451 --> 00:02:07,135
a mile: inflation did change regime. Allow two breaks, and

33
00:02:07,235 --> 00:02:11,833
the hunt finds August nineteen seventy-two and July nineteen eighty-two,

34
00:02:11,933 --> 00:02:15,161
around a decade averaging eight and a half percent.

35
00:02:16,233 --> 00:02:19,875
So: when the break date is found by searching, the evidence must

36
00:02:19,975 --> 00:02:23,150
be judged against the best of all the looks we took. The

37
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:27,653
maximum of many chi squared statistics needs its own, higher, critical value.

